human aging refers in holy grail study  scientists say human aging process  biologically refers in world fast  right so hello and welcome to the chicky  science show where in this video we're  going to talk about the recent article  that has stimulated  both of these and many other news  articles and we're going to go straight  into the data of this article  and pretty much show why these claims  are definitely over-hyped  and cannot be said from the current  findings  and then we'll talk about why it's so  important to speak out about this  because it really does hurt scientists  by spreading misinformation  so let's jump straight into it then the  study that these articles were referring  to  it's this recent ageing article  hyperbaric  oxygen therapy increases telomere length  and decreases immunosine essence in  isolated blood cells  a prospective trial and this study has  come from a team of israeli  scientists led by professor shea efratti  so what did they do in this study and  what did they actually find out  well the lab are interested in  understanding the physiological effects  of hyperbaric oxygen therapy  this therapy involves breathing pure  oxygen  in a pressurized chamber such that it is  higher than the atmospheric pressure  hence hyperbaric and so this is why it  differs to  normoboric oxygen therapy which is the  type of oxygen that's commonly used in  hospitals and clinics for patients with  lung or heart disease  and hyperbaric oxygen treatment can be  given in two settings  mono place and multi-place whereby mono  place  involves just one person in a single  chamber  where the entire chamber is pressurized  with 100 oxygen  however in the study here they use the  multi-place setting  whereby the chamber is still pressurized  with compressed air  but the patient's individually breathe  100 oxygen for your masks  and so the images you're seeing now  should be very similar to what was used  in this study  and the reason that they use high  pressure comes down to henry's law  that states that the amount of molecular  oxygen dissolved in a solution  so a blood or a tissue is directly  proportional to its partial pressure  so basically by using a hyperbaric high  pressure condition  it enhances the amount of oxygen  dissolved in the body's tissues  and so the same lab reported earlier  this year that by using hyperbaric  oxygen therapy  it induced cognitive enhancements in  healthy adults  involving a mechanism that increased  cerebral blood flow  and so in this study they were more  interested in looking at whether or not  hyperbaric oxygen treatment could also  affect telomere length and the number of  senescent cells  in particular the t cells in aging  adults  so after excluding five participants for  various reasons  30 patients received the therapy and  were assessed  and all these patients were healthy and  they were aged  64 and over and it included both male  and females  and so each of these participants  received 60 daily  hyperbaric oxygen therapy exposures  and so each of these sessions included  breathing 100 oxygen by mask  at two atmospheric pressures for 90  minutes with  5 minute air breaks every 20 minutes and  whole blood samples were taken at the  baseline that's the 30th session  and the 60th session and also one to two  weeks following the last session  and then from this blood they looked at  the peripheral blood mononuclear cells  to assess the telomere length and  senescence  and the reason these two traits were  chosen is because  shortening of telomere length and  telling us in essence  are two of the key hallmarks of aging  telomeres are repetitive dna sequences  that are found  at the end of chromosomes and they act  to maintain genome stability  however as the cell divides they can  shorten over time  and as they shorten it increases the  risk of genomic instability  and so it can cause a cell to enter  replicative senescence  whereby that cell no longer divides and  whilst this is useful  for preventing the over-replication of  cancerous cells  the build-up of senescent cells can  result in chronic inflammation  if the senescent cells are not cleared  due to the fact that these cells can  secrete  a variety of different inflammatory  factors and as i've mentioned in  a few videos before removal of senescent  cells  in most models has been shown to have  beneficial impacts for their health  so let's start with the results  regarding the telomere length  as you can see in these figures here the  telomere lumps of  t helper t cytotoxic natural killer  and b cells all increased significantly  by over 20  following the therapy the most  significant being the increases seen in  the b  cells and so this is interesting however  as pointed out by steve hill  from the life extension advocacy  foundation  and their recent article correctly  stated that t-cells have highly dynamic  telomeres  and they can fluctuate considerably  depending on their situation  for example i reported earlier this year  that telomeres  in peripheral blood mononuclear cells  were lengthened during space flight  and so whilst there's nothing wrong with  the data that they collected  it doesn't necessarily give us any  information regarding the ability to  reverse aging  so now let's look at the senescent cell  data well as you can see in these two  graphs here  one to two weeks post the therapy there  was a significant decrease  in the number of t helper and t  cytotoxic  senescent cells and so the authors  concluded that the hyperbaric oxygen  therapy  may induce significant cenolitic effects  however in this case i am a bit curious  as to how they identified senescent  cells  it seems that they only used one marker  which was the absence  of the protein cd28 and so t-cells  aren't really my speciality  but senescence it kind of is and at  least according to this article  absence of cd28 is the suggested marker  of t-cell senescence  along with a couple of other markers but  what they suggest in this study  is that the best way to be able to  characterize senescence within t  cells is to consider these specific t  cell markers  with the more considered classical  markers of cellular senescence  that includes the presence of sab cigar  staining  and the increased expression of p16 and  p21  and markers of this secretory phenotype  referred to  as the sasp and so from their data it  seems like  this hyperbaric oxygen therapy may be  reducing the number of senescent t cells  but i think that more detailed analyses  are definitely required  to know this for certain and the authors  themselves also pointed out a couple of  other limitations within their study for  example a major limitation is the really  small sample size that they had  and the second is a lack of a control  group that obviously weren't treated  with the hyperbaric oxygen therapy and  they also pointed out as i just stated  regarding the lack of markers used to  identify the senescent cells  and moreover they also stated it would  have been good to  have a better understanding of the  long-term impacts of this therapy  treatment  which they weren't able to assess in the  study and to be honest the list could  really continue i think there's tons of  different markers they could have really  tried to  look at for example what about all the  epigenetic changes that occurred  during this treatment i mean  understandably that's the more expensive  thing to do and  just looking at blood and analyzing  t-cells and a couple of  well one marker of senescence is much  easier to do  but anyway i will be interested to look  into any more studies that  do more in-depth analyses regarding the  impacts of hyperbaric oxygen therapy  however the point of this video wasn't  to criticize in any way this publication  in fact i mean there's nothing wrong  with what they've shown  what is wrong is how this data has been  interpreted by the media  for example if we go back to the article  i said at the start of the video  human aging refers to an holy grail  study scientists say  now sounds kind of ridiculous given the  data that i've just shown  and if we just go to the first sentence  scientists claim to have successfully  reversed the biological aging process  in a group of elderly adults i mean i  hope you can at least agree with me that  the data we've seen have shown  just in some blood cells a  potential decline in the number of  senescent cells  and some increases in the telomere  length  but again these are in cells that have  fluctuating telomere lengths  yeah i mean it's just just not just no  i unless you have no words i mean i've  stated before in videos that it's really  hard to try and  define healthspan and to measure it i  mean it's challenging enough to do this  in mice  let alone in humans and given how small  the sample size is  and the lack of data that was acquired  it's just being very overhyped by the  media  and the problem is to create steve again  it only makes it harder for those of us  who are working  earnestly to support progress and it  also once again serves to highlight the  urgent need for journalists to learn to  discern what study results actually mean  instead of hyping stories to sell papers  which is why on this channel  although at times i may confuse you with  too much science  i do try to go into as much detail as i  can with these studies so that you can  really understand  not just what the answer is but why  that's the answer  and so that you can now read these  articles  with your skeptical hat on and  know better anyway i hope this has given  you at least some  better understanding to this hyperbaric  oxygen  therapy treatment paper and the back  backstory  to these over-hyped news articles so i  hope you have learned something in this  video  and as always thanks for listening
 

